# pdca-development-methodology.mdc

meta:
  name: PDCA Development Methodology
  description: >
    Defines the Plan-Do-Check-Act development cycle with test-driven, model-driven approach.
    Ensures systematic problem-solving and quality assurance in all development work.
    Requires tests and logs to exist and fail before implementing any function.
    Enforces strict discipline to prevent scope creep and maintain focus.

rules:
  - when: development.workflow
    then:
      - "Follow PDCA cycle: Plan → Do → Check → Act"
      - "Tests and logs must exist and fail before implementing any function"
      - "Always fix tests first and add logging to make test failures evident"
      - "Use pytest markers to track PDCA phases"
      - "Validate changes against all test suites before completion"
      - "Maintain strict scope discipline - only address identified issues"
      - "Use make commands for all development operations"

  - when: pdca.plan
    then:
      - "Run existing tests to identify failures"
      - "Analyze test output and error messages"
      - "Understand expected vs actual behavior"
      - "Identify root cause of failures"
      - "Document findings and hypothesis"
      - "Define specific scope of work - no scope creep allowed"
      - "Use make test to execute test suite"

  - when: pdca.do
    then:
      - "Read relevant source files"
      - "Examine current implementation state"
      - "Review related test files"
      - "Understand dependencies and context"
      - "Gather all necessary information"
      - "Focus only on identified issues - do not make unrelated changes"
      - "Use make commands for file operations where possible"

  - when: pdca.check
    then:
      - "Validate actual behavior against test expectations"
      - "Verify logging output for error visibility"
      - "Check type annotations and model compliance"
      - "Confirm test coverage requirements"
      - "Ensure all requirements are met"
      - "Verify scope compliance - no unauthorized changes"
      - "Use make test-cov for coverage validation"

  - when: pdca.act
    then:
      - "Implement specific fixes based on analysis"
      - "Add comprehensive logging for debugging"
      - "Update tests if necessary"
      - "Validate changes against all test suites"
      - "Document changes and rationale"
      - "Make minimal changes to address only identified issues"
      - "Use make format and make test for validation"

  - when: test.driven
    then:
      - "Write tests before implementation"
      - "Ensure tests fail initially (red phase)"
      - "Implement minimal code to pass tests (green phase)"
      - "Refactor while maintaining test coverage (refactor phase)"
      - "Maintain test coverage above 75%"
      - "Never implement without failing tests first"
      - "Use make test for test execution"

  - when: logging.required
    then:
      - "Use structlog for structured logging"
      - "Log all error conditions with context"
      - "Make test failures evident through logging"
      - "Include relevant data in log messages"
      - "Use appropriate log levels (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR)"
      - "Add logging before implementing any fixes"

  - when: model.driven
    then:
      - "Use type annotations throughout codebase"
      - "Validate models with mypy"
      - "Follow ontology-driven development patterns"
      - "Ensure semantic consistency"
      - "Document model relationships and constraints"
      - "Use make lint for type checking"

  - when: code.implementation
    then:
      - "Never implement without failing tests first"
      - "Add logging before implementing fixes"
      - "Use pytest markers for PDCA phase tracking"
      - "Follow the exact workflow: Plan → Do → Check → Act"
      - "Validate all changes against test suites"
      - "Make minimal, focused changes only"
      - "Use make commands for all operations"

  - when: scope.discipline
    then:
      - "Define specific scope before starting any work"
      - "Only address identified issues - no scope creep"
      - "Do not make unrelated changes or improvements"
      - "Focus on one problem at a time"
      - "Document scope boundaries clearly"
      - "Validate scope compliance before completion"

  - when: tool.usage
    then:
      - "Use make commands for all development operations"
      - "Prefer uv over pip for package management"
      - "Use structured libraries for file operations"
      - "Follow tool transformation model"
      - "Validate tool outputs before proceeding"

  - when: change.control
    then:
      - "Make minimal changes to address only identified issues"
      - "Do not refactor unrelated code"
      - "Do not add new features unless explicitly requested"
      - "Focus on fixing what's broken, not improving what works"
      - "Document all changes with clear rationale"

workflow:
  pdca_cycle:
    plan:
      - action: "run_tests"
        description: "Execute existing test suite"
        expected: "Identify failing tests and error patterns"
        command: "make test"
      - action: "analyze_failures"
        description: "Understand what's broken"
        expected: "Clear understanding of root cause"
      - action: "document_hypothesis"
        description: "Record findings and proposed solution"
        expected: "Written hypothesis for fix"
      - action: "define_scope"
        description: "Define specific scope of work"
        expected: "Clear scope boundaries"

    do:
      - action: "read_source_files"
        description: "Examine relevant code"
        expected: "Understanding of current implementation"
      - action: "review_tests"
        description: "Analyze test expectations"
        expected: "Clear test requirements"
      - action: "gather_context"
        description: "Understand dependencies and environment"
        expected: "Complete context for implementation"

    check:
      - action: "validate_behavior"
        description: "Compare actual vs expected"
        expected: "Confirmed understanding of issue"
      - action: "verify_logging"
        description: "Ensure error visibility"
        expected: "Comprehensive logging in place"
      - action: "check_coverage"
        description: "Validate test coverage"
        expected: "Coverage requirements met"
        command: "make test-cov"
      - action: "verify_scope"
        description: "Ensure scope compliance"
        expected: "No unauthorized changes"

    act:
      - action: "implement_fix"
        description: "Apply specific solution"
        expected: "Code changes address root cause"
      - action: "add_logging"
        description: "Enhance debugging capability"
        expected: "Clear error visibility"
      - action: "validate_changes"
        description: "Test all affected areas"
        expected: "All tests pass"
        command: "make test"

validation:
  pytest_markers:
    - "pdca_plan: Plan phase tests"
    - "pdca_do: Do phase tests"
    - "pdca_check: Check phase tests"
    - "pdca_act: Act phase tests"
    - "tdd: Test-driven development tests"
    - "logging: Logging behavior tests"
    - "model_driven: Model validation tests"

  requirements:
    test_coverage: ">=75%"
    logging_framework: "structlog"
    type_checking: "mypy"
    test_framework: "pytest"
    coverage_tool: "coverage[toml]"
    scope_discipline: "mandatory"
    tool_usage: "make commands required"

  enforcement:
    scope_control: "strict"
    change_minimization: "mandatory"
    tool_compliance: "strict"
    pdca_phase_completion: "required"

examples:
  pdca_workflow:
    scenario: "Fixing a failing test in authentication module"
    plan:
      - "Run: make test tests/test_authentication.py"
      - "Analyze: Test failure shows missing token validation"
      - "Document: Need to implement token expiry check"
      - "Scope: Only fix token validation, no other changes"
    do:
      - "Read: src/svg_image_generator/authentication.py"
      - "Review: tests/test_authentication.py"
      - "Context: Token validation requirements"
    check:
      - "Validate: Current implementation vs requirements"
      - "Verify: Logging shows token expiry errors"
      - "Coverage: make test-cov to ensure coverage"
      - "Scope: Verify no unrelated changes"
    act:
      - "Implement: Token expiry validation (minimal change)"
      - "Add: Structured logging for token errors"
      - "Test: make test to validate all changes"

  test_driven_example:
    scenario: "Adding new feature with TDD"
    red_phase:
      - "Write failing test for new functionality"
      - "Ensure test captures expected behavior"
      - "Run: make test to confirm it fails"
    green_phase:
      - "Implement minimal code to pass test"
      - "Focus on making test pass, not perfect code"
      - "Verify test now passes with: make test"
    refactor_phase:
      - "Improve code quality while maintaining tests"
      - "Add comprehensive logging"
      - "Ensure all tests still pass: make test"

  scope_discipline_example:
    scenario: "Fixing authentication bug"
    allowed:
      - "Fix token validation logic"
      - "Add logging for token errors"
      - "Update related tests"
    forbidden:
      - "Refactor unrelated authentication methods"
      - "Add new authentication features"
      - "Change other modules"
      - "Improve code style of working code"

  tool_compliance_example:
    scenario: "Development workflow"
    commands:
      - "make test - Run test suite"
      - "make test-cov - Run tests with coverage"
      - "make lint - Check code quality"
      - "make format - Format code"
      - "make build - Build package"
    forbidden:
      - "Direct pytest calls (use make test)"
      - "Direct pip install (use uv)"
      - "Manual file operations (use structured tools)"

rationale: >
  The PDCA methodology ensures systematic problem-solving and quality assurance.
  Combined with test-driven and model-driven approaches, it provides:
  - Systematic approach to problem-solving
  - Quality assurance through comprehensive testing
  - Error visibility through structured logging
  - Model consistency through type checking
  - Reproducible and auditable development process
  - Reduced debugging time through proactive error handling
  - Strict scope discipline to prevent scope creep
  - Tool compliance to ensure consistent workflows

  This methodology prevents common development pitfalls:
  - Implementing without understanding the problem
  - Missing edge cases in testing
  - Poor error visibility and debugging
  - Inconsistent code quality
  - Lack of systematic approach to fixes
  - Scope creep and unauthorized changes
  - Inconsistent tool usage
  - Excessive changes beyond identified issues

  Recent violations observed:
  - Making unrelated changes beyond scope
  - Not following make command requirements
  - Implementing without proper test-first approach
  - Adding features not requested
  - Refactoring working code unnecessarily

---

This rule ensures all development work follows a systematic, test-driven, model-driven approach with comprehensive logging, validation, and strict scope discipline.
description:
globs:
alwaysApply: false
